WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:13.860
AI risk assessment in an AIMS concerns uncertainty that may affect responsible AI objectives, organizational outcomes, systems, operations, security, quality, compliance, or affected parties.

2
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:22.680
The risk picture for a low-impact document classifier is not the same as for a model that influences credit, employment, safety, or access to services.

3
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:27.980
The method should be repeatable without pretending every AI system has the same risk profile.

4
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:34.800
It should also consider opportunities, such as improving control consistency or reducing harmful manual variation.

5
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.880
A sound assessment begins with context and intended purpose.

6
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:50.940
The team identifies the system, decisions, users, affected parties, assets, data dependencies, supplier dependencies, misuse paths, and operating environment.

7
00:00:51.040 --> 00:01:03.440
It then identifies risk sources and events, possible consequences, existing controls, likelihood, consequence, risk level, risk owner, and comparison against pre-established criteria.

8
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.500
Criteria should exist before results are adjusted to justify a preferred launch decision.

9
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.800
This protects the process from hindsight and commercial pressure.

10
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.920
Risk treatment is broader than adding a technical control.

11
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:35.300
The organization may avoid an activity, reduce likelihood, reduce consequence, change the design, narrow the use case, add human review, improve data, increase testing, add monitoring, allocate responsibilities contractually, accept residual risk through authorization, or discontinue the system.

12
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:44.040
Contract language may allocate duties, but it does not automatically remove the organization's accountability to affected parties, customers, or regulators.

13
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.300
Residual risk should be visible to someone with authority to accept it.

14
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:57.540
Annex A should be treated as a reference set of AI-related control objectives and controls, not a universal checklist copied blindly into every project.

15
00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:07.240
Some controls may not apply in a particular context, some may be implemented in different forms, and some risks may require controls beyond Annex A.

16
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:14.140
The important question is whether control selection is reasoned, documented, implemented, owned, and reviewed.

17
00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:21.140
The Statement of Applicability is the governance record that connects risk treatment to control decisions.

18
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:30.200
It should show which controls are applicable, implementation status, reasons for inclusion, justified exclusions, and any additional controls.

19
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.540
It is not merely an audit table.

20
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:40.480
It is a living bridge between risk assessment, treatment decisions, control operation, residual-risk approval, and evidence.

21
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:45.940
Treatment is complete only when controls operate and their effectiveness is evaluated.

22
00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.720
When a system changes, the record may need to change as well.

23
00:02:49.820 --> 00:03:01.340
The mature AIMS treats risk treatment as a managed commitment: selected controls have owners, residual risk has approval, and evidence shows whether the treatment remains effective.
